{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' .codex/skills/literature-review/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: literature-review\ndescription: Conduct systematic literature reviews using a structured workflow. Use when starting a new research project, surveying a field, or documenting related work for a paper.\n---\n\n# Literature Review\n\nSystematic workflow for conducting comprehensive literature reviews.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- Starting a new research project\n- Surveying a research field\n- Writing the Related Work section of a paper\n- Identifying research gaps\n- Building a comprehensive reading list\n\n## PRISMA-Lite Workflow\n\nThis workflow adapts the PRISMA (Preferred Reporting Items for Systematic Reviews) framework for ML/AI research.\n\n### Phase 1: Define Scope\n\nBefore searching, define:\n\n1. **Research Question**: What are you trying to learn?\n2. **Inclusion Criteria**: What makes a paper relevant?\n3. **Exclusion Criteria**: What makes a paper not relevant?\n4. **Time Frame**: How far back to search?\n5. **Search Sources**: Which databases to use?\n\nDocument in `literature_review.md`:\n\n```markdown\n## Review Scope\n\n### Research Question\n[Your research question here]\n\n### Inclusion Criteria\n- [ ] Criterion 1\n- [ ] Criterion 2\n\n### Exclusion Criteria\n- [ ] Criterion 1\n- [ ] Criterion 2\n\n### Time Frame\n[e.g., 2019-present]\n\n### Sources\n- [ ] Semantic Scholar\n- [ ] arXiv\n- [ ] Google Scholar\n- [ ] ACL Anthology\n```\n\n### Phase 2: Search\n\nExecute systematic search using paper-finder or manual search:\n\n1. **Primary search**: Use core topic terms\n2. **Secondary search**: Use method/technique names\n3. **Citation search**: Check references of key papers\n\nTrack search queries:\n\n```markdown\n## Search Log\n\n| Date | Query | Source | Results | Notes |\n|------|-------|--------|---------|-------|\n| YYYY-MM-DD | \"query here\" | Semantic Scholar | N papers | Initial search |\n```\n\n### Phase 3: Screening\n\nThree-stage screening process:\n\n#### Stage 1: Title Screening\n- Review all titles\n- Quick relevance judgment\n- Mark: Include / Exclude / Maybe\n\n#### Stage 2: Abstract Screening\n- Read abstracts for Include/Maybe papers\n- Evaluate methodology and findings\n- Mark: Include / Exclude\n\n#### Stage 3: Full-Text Screening\n- Download and read full papers\n- Verify relevance and quality\n- Extract key information\n\nTrack screening:\n\n```markdown\n## Screening Results\n\n| Paper | Title Screen | Abstract Screen | Full-Text | Notes |\n|-------|-------------|-----------------|-----------|-------|\n| Paper1 | Include | Include | Include | Key baseline |\n| Paper2 | Maybe | Exclude | - | Different task |\n```\n\n### Phase 4: Data Extraction\n\nFor each included paper, extract:\n\n1. **Bibliographic**: Authors, year, venue\n2. **Problem**: What problem is addressed?\n3. **Method**: What approach is used?\n4. **Data**: What datasets/benchmarks?\n5. **Results**: Key findings\n6. **Limitations**: Acknowledged weaknesses\n7. **Relevance**: How relates to our work?\n\nUse the extraction template in `assets/review_template.md`.\n\n### Phase 5: Synthesis\n\nOrganize findings by theme:\n\n1. **Identify themes**: Group related papers\n2. **Compare approaches**: What are the differences?\n3. **Find gaps**: What's missing?\n4. **Position work**: Where does your work fit?\n\n## Output Files\n\n### literature_review.md\n\nMain document tracking the review:\n\n```markdown\n# Literature Review: [Topic]\n\n## Review Scope\n[Scope definition]\n\n## Search Log\n[Search queries and results]\n\n## Paper Summaries\n[Individual paper notes]\n\n## Themes and Synthesis\n[Grouped findings]\n\n## Research Gaps\n[Identified opportunities]\n\n## Key Citations\n[Must-cite papers for your work]\n```\n\n### papers/ directory\n\nOrganize downloaded papers:\n\n```\npapers/\n├── must_read/           # Relevance 3, priority reading\n├── should_read/         # Relevance 2\n├── reference/           # Background papers\n└── README.md            # Index of all papers\n```\n\n## Tools\n\n### Reading Large PDFs\n\nUse the PDF chunker to split papers into smaller PDF files that can be read directly.\nThis preserves all formatting perfectly (unlike text extraction which loses formatting).\n\n**Dependencies:**\n```bash\n# Using uv (recommended):\nuv add pypdf\n\n# Or with pip:\npip install pypdf\n```\n\n**How to run:**\n\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/literature-review/scripts/pdf_chunker.py <pdf_path>\n```\n\nOptions:\n- `--pages-per-chunk N`: Number of pages per chunk (default: 1)\n- `--output-dir DIR`: Output directory (default: `<pdf_dir>/pages`)\n\n**Output:**\n- Creates PDF chunk files: `<pdf_name>_chunk_001.pdf`, `<pdf_name>_chunk_002.pdf`, etc.\n- Creates a manifest: `<pdf_name>_manifest.txt` listing all chunks with page ranges\n\n**Integration with screening workflow:**\n1. During Phase 3 (Full-Text Screening), run the chunker on papers that need detailed review\n2. For abstract skimming: read only chunk 1 (page 1 or pages 1-3)\n3. For deep reading: read ALL chunk PDFs sequentially, writing notes after each\n4. Check the manifest to see how many chunks exist\n5. IMPORTANT: Do not skip chunks - methodology and results are in later chunks\n\n### Verify Citations\n\nAfter completing the review, verify all citations are valid:\n\n```bash\npython .claude/skills/literature-review/scripts/verify_citations.py literature_review.md\n```\n\n## Quality Checklist\n\n- [ ] Research question clearly defined\n- [ ] Inclusion/exclusion criteria documented\n- [ ] Multiple sources searched\n- [ ] Search queries logged\n- [ ] Screening decisions recorded\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' resources.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Resources Catalog\n\n## Summary\nThis document catalogs all resources gathered for the project on self-critique-induced representation drift.\n\n## Papers\nTotal papers downloaded: 10\n\n| Title | Authors | Year | File | Key Info |\n|-------|---------|------|------|----------|\n| Reflexion | Shinn et al. | 2023 | papers/2303.11366_reflexion.pdf | verbal RL/self-reflection loop |\n| Self-Refine | Madaan et al. | 2023 | papers/2303.17651_self_refine.pdf | iterative self-feedback |\n| Quiet-STaR | Zelikman et al. | 2024 | papers/2403.09629_quiet_star.pdf | latent thought training |\n| Math-Shepherd | Wang et al. | 2023 | papers/2312.08935_math_shepherd.pdf | step-level verifier reward |\n| Better Reasoners with Alignment | Wang et al. | 2023 | papers/2309.02144_reasoners_with_alignment.pdf | alignment improves reasoning |\n| Reasoning via Steering Vectors | Venhoff et al. | 2025 | papers/2506.18167_reasoning_via_steering_vectors.pdf | activation-level behavior control |\n| Depth-Wise Activation Steering | Góral et al. | 2025 | papers/2512.07667_depth_wise_activation_steering.pdf | depth-scheduled steering |\n| TruthfulQA | Lin et al. | 2021 | papers/2109.07958_truthfulqa.pdf | truthfulness benchmark |\n| Training Verifiers | Cobbe et al. | 2021 | papers/2110.14168_training_verifiers_math_word_problems.pdf | GSM8K + verifier framework |\n| Tree of Thoughts | Yao et al. | 2023 | papers/2305.10601_tree_of_thoughts.pdf | deliberate search baseline |\n\nSee `papers/README.md` for details.\n\n## Datasets\nTotal datasets downloaded: 3\n\n| Name | Source | Size | Task | Location | Notes |\n|------|--------|------|------|----------|-------|\n| GSM8K | HuggingFace | 7,473 train / 1,319 test | math reasoning | datasets/gsm8k/ | primary correctness benchmark |\n| TruthfulQA (generation) | HuggingFace | 817 val | truthfulness QA | datasets/truthful_qa_generation/ | stress test for hallucination resistance |\n| CommonsenseQA | HuggingFace | 9,741 train / 1,221 val / 1,140 test | MCQ commonsense reasoning | datasets/commonsense_qa/ | out-of-domain reasoning check |\n\nSee `datasets/README.md` for download and load instructions.\n\n## Code Repositories\nTotal repositories cloned: 4\n\n| Name | URL | Purpose | Location | Notes |\n|------|-----|---------|----------|-------|\n| reflexion | https://github.com/noahshinn/reflexion | self-reflection agents | code/reflexion/ | HotPotQA/HumanEval/ALFWorld scripts |\n| self-refine | https://github.com/madaan/self-refine | iterative self-feedback | code/self-refine/ | includes GSM task scripts |\n| quiet-star | https://github.com/ezelikman/quiet-star | latent thought training | code/quiet-star/ | transformer patching implementation |\n| tree-of-thought-llm | https://github.com/princeton-nlp/tree-of-thought-llm | deliberate search baseline | code/tree-of-thought-llm/ | BFS/DFS ToT framework |\n\nSee `code/README.md` for key files and usage notes.\n\n## Resource Gathering Notes\n\n### Search Strategy\n- Attempted paper-finder diligent query first; service did not return output in this run.\n- Performed fallback targeted arXiv searches for self-critique, verifier, and activation-steering papers.\n- Prioritized papers with direct method relevance and available code/data links.\n\n### Selection Criteria\n- Directly tests self-critique/reflection or process verification.\n- Enables activation-space or residual-stream style analysis.\n- Provides benchmark datasets or widely used evaluation settings.\n\n### Challenges Encountered\n- paper-finder script returned no output (likely backend/service issue).\n- one candidate GitHub repo URL was unavailable (404).\n\n### Gaps and Workarounds\n- Limited public code for newest activation-analysis papers.\n- Workaround: combine available implementations (`quiet-star`, `reflexion`, `self-refine`, `tree-of-thought-llm`) with custom probing scripts in the experiment phase.\n\n## Recommendations for Experiment Design\n1. **Primary dataset(s)**: GSM8K + CommonsenseQA, with TruthfulQA as robustness set.\n2. **Baseline methods**: single-pass CoT, Self-Refine, Reflexion memory loop, Tree-of-Thought.\n3. **Evaluation metrics**: accuracy/EM/pass@1 plus probe AUC and representation drift measures (CKA/SVCCA).\n4. **Code to adapt/reuse**: start from `code/self-refine/src/gsm/run.py` and `code/reflexion/hotpotqa_runs/` patterns; use ToT repo as deliberate search comparator.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' .codex/skills/critical-analysis/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: critical-analysis\ndescription: Evaluate research quality using frameworks like GRADE, bias detection, and logical analysis. Use when assessing paper quality, evaluating claims, or reviewing evidence strength.\n---\n\n# Critical Analysis\n\nFrameworks for evaluating research quality and evidence strength.\n\n## When to Use\n\n- Assessing quality of papers during literature review\n- Evaluating strength of claims\n- Identifying biases in research\n- Reviewing your own work critically\n- Preparing rebuttals or responses\n\n## GRADE Framework (Adapted for ML)\n\nGRADE (Grading of Recommendations, Assessment, Development and Evaluations) adapted for ML research.\n\n### Evidence Quality Levels\n\n| Level | Definition | ML Example |\n|-------|------------|------------|\n| **High** | Very confident the true effect is close to estimate | Large-scale, well-designed study with multiple benchmarks, ablations, statistical significance |\n| **Moderate** | Moderately confident; true effect likely close to estimate | Good methodology with some limitations in scope or evaluation |\n| **Low** | Limited confidence; true effect may be substantially different | Significant methodological concerns or limited evaluation |\n| **Very Low** | Very little confidence; true effect likely substantially different | Major flaws or insufficient evidence |\n\n### Factors That Lower Quality\n\n| Factor | Description | Example |\n|--------|-------------|---------|\n| **Risk of bias** | Methodological flaws | Unfair baseline comparisons, p-hacking |\n| **Inconsistency** | Results vary across conditions | Works on one dataset but not others |\n| **Indirectness** | Evidence doesn't match question | Tested on different task than claimed |\n| **Imprecision** | Wide confidence intervals | Single run, no error bars |\n| **Publication bias** | Selective reporting | Only positive results shown |\n\n### Factors That Raise Quality\n\n| Factor | Description | Example |\n|--------|-------------|---------|\n| **Large effect** | Clear, substantial improvement | >10% improvement over SOTA |\n| **Dose-response** | Consistent relationship | Improvement scales with X |\n| **Confounds addressed** | Alternative explanations ruled out | Ablations, controls |\n\n### Assessment Template\n\n```markdown\n## GRADE Assessment: [Paper]\n\n### Initial Rating\n[ ] High (well-designed study)\n[ ] Moderate (some limitations)\n[ ] Low (significant concerns)\n[ ] Very Low (major flaws)\n\n### Downgrade Factors\n- [ ] Risk of bias: [Description]\n- [ ] Inconsistency: [Description]\n- [ ] Indirectness: [Description]\n- [ ] Imprecision: [Description]\n- [ ] Publication bias: [Description]\n\n### Upgrade Factors\n- [ ] Large effect: [Description]\n- [ ] Dose-response: [Description]\n- [ ] Confounds addressed: [Description]\n\n### Final Rating\n[Level] because [reasoning]\n```\n\n## Bias Detection\n\n### Types of Bias in ML Research\n\n| Bias Type | Description | How to Detect |\n|-----------|-------------|---------------|\n| **Selection bias** | Non-representative data/models | Check data sources, model selection criteria |\n| **Confirmation bias** | Favoring supporting evidence | Look for missing negative results |\n| **Survivorship bias** | Only successful examples shown | Ask about failed attempts |\n| **Anchoring bias** | Over-relying on initial results | Check if conclusions change with more data |\n| **Availability bias** | Using easily accessible options | Are baselines convenient or appropriate? |\n\n### Experimental Bias Checklist\n\n- [ ] **Data leakage**: Test data seen during training?\n- [ ] **Hyperparameter selection**: Tuned on test set?\n- [ ] **Baseline fairness**: Same compute budget for baselines?\n- [ ] **Cherry-picking**: Best-case results only?\n- [ ] **P-hacking**: Multiple tests without correction?\n- [ ] **HARKing**: Hypotheses generated after results?\n\n### Reporting Bias Checklist\n\n- [ ] **Selective metrics**: Only favorable metrics shown?\n- [ ] **Selective datasets**: Only favorable datasets?\n- [ ] **Selective examples**: Only successful cases?\n- [ ] **Buried failures**: Limitations minimized?\n\n## Statistical Validity\n\n### Common Statistical Issues\n\n| Issue | Description | Red Flag |\n|-------|-------------|----------|\n| **No significance testing** | No p-values or confidence intervals | \"Method A is better\" with no stats |\n| **Multiple comparisons** | Many tests without correction | Many experiments, no Bonferroni |\n| **Single run** | No variance estimate | Results without ± |\n| **Wrong test** | Inappropriate statistical test | Paired test for unpaired data |\n| **Misinterpretation** | Conflating significance and importance | \"Statistically significant\" but tiny effect |\n\n### Statistical Checklist\n\n- [ ] Sample size appropriate\n- [ ] Multiple runs with different seeds\n- [ ] Error bars or confidence intervals\n- [ ] Appropriate statistical test used\n- [ ] Multiple comparison correction (if applicable)\n- [ ] Effect size reported (not just p-values)\n\n## Logical Fallacy Detection\n\n### Common Fallacies in ML Papers\n\n| Fallacy | Description | Example |\n|---------|-------------|---------|\n| **Appeal to novelty** | New = better | \"Our novel method...\" |\n| **Appeal to complexity** | Complex = better | More parameters = better |\n| **False dichotomy** | Only two options | \"Either scale or architecture\" |\n| **Hasty generalization** | Few examples → general claim | One dataset → \"generally better\" |\n| **Moving goalposts** | Changing success criteria | Switching metrics when losing |\n| **Straw man** | Misrepresenting baseline | Weakened baseline comparison |\n| **Texas sharpshooter** | Pattern from randomness | Post-hoc metric selection |\n\n### Argument Analysis\n\nFor each major claim:\n1. **Identify the claim**: What exactly is being stated?\n2. **Find the evidence**: What supports it?\n3. **Evaluate the link**: Does evidence actually support claim?\n4. **Check assumptions**: What's taken for granted?\n5. **Consider alternatives**: What else could explain this?\n\n## Critical Questions by Section\n\n### For Claims\n- Is this claim clearly stated?\n- Is it testable?\n- What evidence supports it?\n- What would falsify it?\n- Are there alternative explanations?\n\n### For Methods\n- Could this be reproduced?\n- Are assumptions stated?\n- Are design choices justified?\n- What are the limitations?\n\n### For Experiments\n- Are baselines appropriate?\n- Is evaluation fair?\n- Is variance reported?\n- Are ablations thorough?\n- Could results be cherry-picked?\n\n### For Results\n- Do results support claims?\n- Are effects meaningful (not just significant)?\n- Do results generalize?\n- Are negative results reported?\n\n## Critical Reading Template\n\n```markdown\n## Critical Analysis: [Paper Title]\n\n### Main Claims\n1. [Claim 1]\n2. [Claim 2]\n3. [Claim 3]\n\n### Evidence Assessment\n\n| Claim | Evidence | Strength | Issues |\n|-------|----------|----------|--------|\n| [Claim 1] | [Evidence] | [Strong/Moderate/Weak] | [Issues] |\n\n### Methodology Concerns\n- [ ] [Concern 1]\n- [ ] [Concern 2]\n\n### Statistical Concerns\n- [ ] [Concern 1]\n- [ ] [Concern 2]\n\n### Potential Biases\n- [ ] [Bias 1]\n- [ ] [Bias 2]\n\n### Alternative Explanations\n1. [Alternative 1]\n2. [Alternative 2]\n\n### Overall Assessment\n**Quality**: [High/Moderate/Low/Very Low]\n**Confidence in claims**: [High/Medium/Low]\n**Key limitation**: [Most important issue]\n```\n\n## Quality Checklist\n\nWhen critically analyzing any work:\n\n- [ ] Claims are clearly identified\n- [ ] Evidence for each claim is assessed\n- [ ] Methodology is scrutinized\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' literature_review.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Literature Review: Representation Drift Under Self-Reflection\n\n## Review Scope\n\n### Research Question\nDoes self-critique/self-reflection in LLM inference induce measurable, structured shifts in internal representations (especially residual stream states) that align with improved reasoning correctness?\n\n### Inclusion Criteria\n- Introduces explicit self-critique, self-refinement, verifier, or process-level reasoning control.\n- Includes reasoning benchmarks with correctness metrics.\n- Includes activation-level analysis or intervention relevant to representation geometry.\n\n### Exclusion Criteria\n- Purely application-specific agent papers without methodological transfer value.\n- Non-LLM or non-reasoning-focused works.\n\n### Time Frame\n- Primarily 2021-2025 (plus benchmark foundations from 2021).\n\n### Sources\n- Paper-finder script (attempted; service non-responsive in this run)\n- arXiv API fallback search\n- GitHub official repos from selected papers\n\n## Search Log\n\n| Date | Query | Source | Results | Notes |\n|------|-------|--------|---------|-------|\n| 2026-03-08 | representation drift self-reflection residual stream | paper-finder | timeout/no output | used manual fallback |\n| 2026-03-08 | Reflexion / Self-Refine / Quiet-STaR / steering vectors | arXiv API | 10 included | downloaded full PDFs |\n| 2026-03-08 | official implementation repos | GitHub | 4 cloned | 1 repo URL not found |\n\n## Screening Results\n\n| Paper | Title Screen | Abstract Screen | Full-Text | Notes |\n|------|--------------|-----------------|----------|-------|\n| 2303.11366 | Include | Include | Include (deep) | core self-reflection method |\n| 2303.17651 | Include | Include | Include (deep) | direct self-feedback loop |\n| 2403.09629 | Include | Include | Include (deep) | latent thinking before speaking |\n| 2506.18167 | Include | Include | Include (deep) | activation steering analysis |\n| 2512.07667 | Include | Include | Skim | depth-wise steering for honesty |\n| 2312.08935 | Include | Include | Skim | process-level verification |\n| 2309.02144 | Include | Include | Skim | alignment for better reasoning |\n| 2305.10601 | Include | Include | Skim | deliberate search baseline |\n| 2110.14168 | Include | Include | Skim | verifier framework + GSM8K |\n| 2109.07958 | Include | Include | Skim | truthfulness benchmark |\n\n## Key Papers\n\n### Paper: Reflexion: Language Agents with Verbal Reinforcement Learning\n- Authors: Noah Shinn et al.\n- Year: 2023\n- Source: arXiv / NeurIPS 2023 context\n- Key Contribution: verbal reinforcement loop using self-reflection memory across trials.\n- Methodology: actor + evaluator + self-reflection memory; iterative trial-based updates without weight updates.\n- Datasets Used: HotPotQA, HumanEval, ALFWorld, WebShop.\n- Baselines: ReAct, CoT variants, non-reflective retries.\n- Results: strong gains in pass@1 / task success across environments from reflective memory.\n- Code: https://github.com/noahshinn/reflexion\n- Relevance: primary operational definition of self-critique-induced behavior change.\n\n### Paper: Self-Refine: Iterative Refinement with Self-Feedback\n- Authors: Aman Madaan et al.\n- Year: 2023\n- Source: arXiv\n- Key Contribution: FEEDBACK->REFINE loop where the same model critiques and improves outputs iteratively.\n- Methodology: prompt templates for initialization, feedback, iterate; repeated inference passes.\n- Datasets Used: GSM8K plus multiple generation tasks.\n- Baselines: one-shot generation and direct instruction baselines.\n- Results: iterative improvements across tasks and human preference gains.\n- Code: https://github.com/madaan/self-refine\n- Relevance: direct template for constructing pre/post reflection activation comparisons.\n\n### Paper: Quiet-STaR\n- Authors: Eric Zelikman et al.\n- Year: 2024\n- Source: arXiv\n- Key Contribution: trains models to produce useful latent thoughts before output, extending STaR principles.\n- Methodology: inserted thought tokens and objective shaping for implicit reasoning.\n- Datasets Used: CommonsenseQA, GSM8K, additional reasoning data.\n- Baselines: standard language-model training/inference variants.\n- Results: better reasoning under latent thought regimes.\n- Code: https://github.com/ezelikman/quiet-star\n- Relevance: strong conceptual bridge between internal thought structure and external answer quality.\n\n### Paper: Understanding Reasoning in Thinking Language Models via Steering Vectors\n- Authors: Constantin Venhoff et al.\n- Year: 2025\n- Source: ICLR 2025 workshop paper (arXiv)\n- Key Contribution: identifies behavior-specific steering vectors and manipulates reasoning sections.\n- Methodology: activation extraction, behavioral section labeling, positive/negative steering.\n- Datasets Used: 500 tasks across 10 categories (includes ARC-style tasks).\n- Baselines: unsteered model behavior.\n- Results: targeted steering changes reasoning style and can affect accuracy.\n- Code: not standardized official benchmark code, but methods are reproducible from paper.\n- Relevance: directly supports residual-stream/subspace hypotheses.\n\n### Other Included Papers (Quick Notes)\n- **Math-Shepherd (2312.08935)**: process reward model for step-level verification; useful for correctness separability labels.\n- **Reasoners with Alignment (2309.02144)**: alignment procedures can improve reasoning quality.\n- **Depth-Wise Activation Steering (2512.07667)**: depth-scheduled steering improves honesty, suggesting layer-dependent representation control.\n- **Tree of Thoughts (2305.10601)**: search-based inference baseline for structured reasoning.\n- **Training Verifiers (2110.14168)**: verifier-guided selection and GSM8K benchmark foundation.\n- **TruthfulQA (2109.07958)**: benchmark for truthfulness vs imitation of falsehoods.\n\n## Common Methodologies\n- Iterative self-critique loops: Reflexion, Self-Refine.\n- Process-level verification/rewarding: Math-Shepherd, Training Verifiers.\n- Internal representation intervention: Steering vectors, depth-wise activation steering.\n- Deliberate search inference: Tree of Thoughts.\n\n## Standard Baselines\n- Direct CoT (single pass).\n- ReAct-style agent without memory.\n- Best-of-N / verifier reranking.\n- ToT BFS/DFS deliberate search.\n- No-steering internal-state baseline for activation studies.\n\n## Evaluation Metrics\n- Final-answer accuracy (GSM8K, CommonsenseQA).\n- Exact Match / Pass@1 (reasoning/program synthesis settings).\n- Truthfulness / honesty rates (TruthfulQA, MASK-like honesty benchmarks).\n- Step-level correctness proxies (process reward / verifier scores).\n- Representation metrics to add in experiments: CKA/SVCCA drift, linear separability AUC, manifold compactness.\n\n## Datasets in the Literature\n- GSM8K: self-refinement and verifier work.\n- CommonsenseQA: reasoning generalization (Quiet-STaR).\n- TruthfulQA: truthfulness vs plausible falsehood behavior.\n- HotPotQA/HumanEval/ALFWorld: Reflexion evaluation suites.\n\n## Gaps and Opportunities\n- Few papers jointly measure activation drift + correctness gains during self-critique loops.\n- Most internal-state studies steer behaviors but do not quantify manifold convergence across iterations.\n- Need controlled pre/post critique probes at multiple residual layers with matched prompts.\n\n## Recommendations for Our Experiment\n- Recommended datasets:\n  - GSM8K (primary correctness + reasoning traces)\n  - CommonsenseQA (cross-domain robustness)\n  - TruthfulQA (truthfulness-oriented stress test)\n- Recommended baselines:\n  - single-pass CoT\n  - Self-Refine loop\n  - Reflexion-style memory loop\n  - ToT search baseline\n- Recommended metrics:\n  - accuracy/EM/pass@1\n  - linear probe AUC for correct vs incorrect reasoning states\n  - CKA/SVCCA/CCA drift across iterations\n  - subspace overlap and trajectory stability metrics in residual stream\n- Methodological considerations:\n  - keep decoding temperature controlled across variants.\n  - pair each question’s iteration states for within-instance drift analysis.\n  - evaluate both early and late residual layers to detect depth-localized drift.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
