{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/usr/bin/bash -lc \"cat > planning.md << 'EOF'\n# Planning: Representation Drift Under Self-Reflection\n\n## Motivation & Novelty Assessment\n\n### Why This Research Matters\nSelf-critique prompting is widely used to improve LLM outputs, but current evidence is mostly behavioral (accuracy gains) rather than mechanistic. If reflection actually reorganizes internal computation, that would provide a measurable notion of cognitive change and better guidance for recursive self-improvement agents. This study links practical prompting gains to residual-stream dynamics, improving interpretability and reliability claims.\n\n### Gap in Existing Work\nPrior work (Reflexion, Self-Refine) shows output improvements from iterative critique but does not rigorously quantify residual-stream geometry changes across critique rounds. Activation-steering work demonstrates controllability of behavior but does not directly test whether self-critique naturally induces stable reasoning manifolds tied to correctness. The literature lacks a unified pre/post reflection protocol combining performance, drift metrics, and linear separability of correct reasoning.\n\n### Our Novel Contribution\nWe test whether self-critique causes structured representational drift (not just random perturbation) by combining: (1) within-question activation trajectory analysis across rounds, (2) layer-wise drift and manifold-structure metrics, and (3) correctness-linked probe separability. We also compare single-round self-critique, multi-round self-critique, and externally supplied critique to isolate whether reflection quality and source matter.\n\n### Experiment Justification\n- Experiment 1: Behavior + drift under single-round self-critique.\n  - Why needed: establishes the core pre/post effect with minimal confounds.\n- Experiment 2: Multi-round reflection trajectories.\n  - Why needed: tests convergence/stability of internal states over repeated refinement.\n- Experiment 3: Self-critique vs external/human-like critique.\n  - Why needed: distinguishes generic revision effects from self-generated reflection effects.\n- Experiment 4: Model-size comparison (small vs larger open models).\n  - Why needed: checks scaling dependence of representational restructuring.\n\n## Research Question\nDoes self-critique in LLM inference induce structured, correctness-aligned shifts in internal residual-stream representations, rather than shallow re-sampling near the original activation neighborhood?\n\n## Background and Motivation\nSelf-reflection methods improve reasoning outcomes across datasets, but mechanistic evidence remains sparse. Existing papers support two partial views: iterative prompting improves outputs, and residual activations can be steered to alter behavior. This project unifies these lines by directly measuring representation drift geometry while tracking answer correctness.\n\n## Hypothesis Decomposition\n- H1 (Drift Magnitude): Post-critique residual states differ significantly from pre-critique states (layer-wise CKA decrease / cosine drift increase).\n- H2 (Structured Drift): Drift is non-random and aligns with improved geometry (higher separability of correct vs incorrect states; lower within-class dispersion for correct states).\n- H3 (Convergence): Multi-round critique trajectories converge toward a stable manifold (decreasing step-to-step drift with retained correctness gains).\n- H4 (Specificity): Self-critique induces stronger structured drift than paraphrase-only or generic rewrite controls.\n\nIndependent variables:\n- Prompting condition: direct answer, single self-critique, multi-round self-critique, external critique.\n- Round index: 0..R.\n- Model size/family.\n\nDependent variables:\n- Task accuracy / exact match.\n- Layer-wise drift metrics (cosine distance, CKA, Procrustes distance).\n- Probe metrics (AUC/F1 for correctness classification from activations).\n- Manifold metrics (silhouette score, within-class covariance trace, trajectory curvature).\n\nAlternative explanations and controls:\n- Alternative: gains are due to extra tokens/time, not reflection quality.\n  - Control: equal-length rewrite baseline with no critique intent.\n- Alternative: random decoding causes drift.\n  - Control: low temperature deterministic runs and fixed seeds.\n\n## Proposed Methodology\n\n### Approach\nUse real LLMs with accessible hidden states from Hugging Face causal models for mechanistic metrics, and optionally API models for behavioral triangulation. Primary mechanistic experiments run on local open models to extract residual streams at each layer for each prompt round.\n\n### Experimental Steps\n1. Build evaluation subset from pre-downloaded GSM8K, CommonsenseQA, TruthfulQA (balanced, >=50 items per set for statistical validity).\n2. Implement prompting pipelines for 4 conditions: direct, self-critique (1 round), self-critique (3 rounds), external critique template.\n3. Capture residual stream activations (final token and pooled sequence representations) at each layer per round.\n4. Compute behavioral metrics and drift/manifold metrics per sample and aggregate by condition.\n5. Train linear probes on held-out split to predict correctness from activations (pre vs post).\n6. Run statistical tests (paired tests, effect sizes, multiple-testing correction).\n7. Perform error analysis and robustness checks across seeds and datasets.\n\n### Baselines\n- Baseline A: Direct CoT single-pass response.\n- Baseline B: Equal-token rewrite (no critique intent).\n- Baseline C: External critique then revise.\n- Comparator: Self-critique (1 and 3 rounds).\n\n### Evaluation Metrics\nBehavioral:\n- Accuracy / EM (GSM8K, CommonsenseQA), truthful score proxy (TruthfulQA MC-style scoring where available).\n\nRepresentational:\n- Layer-wise cosine drift: mean distance between round-0 and round-r residual vectors.\n- CKA similarity between round-0 and round-r activation matrices.\n- Probe AUC/F1 for correctness from residual features.\n- Manifold compactness/separation: covariance trace, silhouette score, inter/intra-class distance ratio.\n- Trajectory stability: round-to-round drift decay slope.\n\n### Statistical Analysis Plan\n- Primary tests: paired t-test or Wilcoxon signed-rank (normality-dependent) for within-question condition comparisons.\n- Effect sizes: Cohen's d (paired) and Cliff's delta where non-parametric.\n- Confidence intervals: bootstrap 95% CIs (1,000 resamples).\n- Multiple comparisons: Benjamini-Hochberg FDR q=0.05 across layers/metrics.\n- Significance level: alpha=0.05 (two-sided).\n\n## Expected Outcomes\nSupport for hypothesis:\n- Significant accuracy improvement for self-critique conditions.\n- Significant structured drift (moderate+ effect sizes) concentrated in mid/late layers.\n- Improved probe separability and increased manifold structure for correct responses post-critique.\n- Multi-round trajectories show partial convergence (decreasing incremental drift).\n\nRefutation pattern:\n- Minimal drift despite performance changes or drift indistinguishable from rewrite control.\n- No probe separability gains or no convergence trend.\n\n## Timeline and Milestones\n- M1 (setup/data checks): 20 min\n- M2 (pipeline + activation capture): 60 min\n- M3 (experiments all conditions): 60 min\n- M4 (analysis + plots + stats): 45 min\n- M5 (documentation + validation): 30 min\n- Buffer/debugging: 30 min\n\n## Potential Challenges\n- Limited compute for larger models: use smaller instruction-tuned models and reduced subset first.\n- Hidden-state extraction memory overhead: process in mini-batches and save intermediate tensors.\n- Dataset answer parsing noise: implement robust normalization and fallback regex parsing.\n- Confounding from generation randomness: deterministic decoding + repeated-seed robustness runs.\n\n## Success Criteria\n- End-to-end reproducible pipeline with saved config, metrics, and plots.\n- At least one dataset shows statistically significant structured drift under self-critique vs controls.\n- Report includes behavioral gains/losses, drift statistics, probe results, and failure analysis.\nEOF\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
